===Etymology===
{{etydate|1890}} Borrowed from {{bor|en|de|Wurst||sausage, wurst}}, from {{der|en|gmh|wurst}}, from {{der|en|goh|wurst}}, from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*wursti||something turned or twisted}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*wert-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*werd-||to turn}}. Akin to {{cog|osx|worst||wurst}}, {{cog|ang|weorþan||to turn, become}}. {{doublet|en|wors}}. Unrelated to {{m|en|worsted||type of yarn}}. More at {{m|en|worth||to be, become, betide}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/wɝst/|/vɝst/|/vʊɹst/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/wɜːst/|/vɜːst/|/vʊəst/}}
* {{homophones|en|worst}} {{qualifier|one pronunciation}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜː(ɹ)st|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[German]]- or [[Austrian]]-style [[sausage]].
#* '''2011''', Dardis McNamee, ''Frommer's Austria''
#*: In summer, you're welcomed into a flower-decked garden set against a backdrop of ancient vineyards. You can fill up your platter with some of the best '''wursts''' and roast meats (especially the delectable pork), along with freshly made salads.

